Z||000000||falconhulk||08-20-2001||11:53 AM||||I have a few wierd problems with my fiero.  One is with the breaks.  I thought it was a fluke last week after driving about 40 miles my breaks started going out!  The pedal went all the way to the floor.  I was able to pump them up but it was dangerous.  if I hit the gas again I had to pump again.  As long as I did not touch the gas the breaks were fine.  Then in like 4 minutes it was fine.  The samething happened yesterday and all of a sudden it stopped.  I checked the master cylinder and it was fine.  I drove it a little this morning and it was fine.  It was not like the break went all the way to the floor and the car would not slow down...it was like it did not grab at all.  Yesterday the first time it went all the way down the break light came on.  It did not come on after that.  I cannot see any leaks of any problem.  The master cylinder looks brand new.  I am going to take it to the dealer for another wierd problem but am worried about this problem now.  Yesterday I drove it for 80 miles and it did not do this until I was 3 miles from home.  Any ideas?  Looking at the manual it says that maybe the wrong fluid was used?  I guess that it may be boiling?  I do live on a mountain and had just come down some good slopes but I dont use the break much at all.  Its very strange becasue its done this on my 2 longest trips with it but its fine when I take it for 10 and 15 mile fun runs.||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||08-20-2001||04:09 PM||||if you are not going through brake fluid and you have intermitent brake problems than replace the master cylender. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-20-2001||07:41 PM||||sounds like a brake over heat.<P>either a caliper is dragging and heating up or a line is heating up. there's a brake line that runs across the trunk wall that would be a posibility, especially if there is an exhaust leak near it.<P>You need to check all the calipers for signs of dragging. (If on is it will probably have baddly worn pads.) <P>After that you need to flush the brake fluid. Crappy old brake fluid is a huge cause of problems with brakes. Old fluid can boil at very low temperatures. Read the brake articles in my cave. Link at top of all forum pages.||||reg||1
